Ray <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
> lmfback wrote:
>
>> In article <gukm3e$rqh$1@news.albasani.net>, kulhanek.5@wright.edu
>> says...
>
>> Valid points, and at this time I certainly agree with you. In the
>> future, however, savefile compability may be broken if we can improve
>> the savefiles/on disk level files to a greater robustness. Although not
>> a common problem for people, allowing for a game to continue past
>> "minor" problems would be a good nod towards the player.
>
> Two observations:  First, the unique items required to win the game
> are sufficiently special that it is worthwhile for a special section
> of the savefile near the beginning to record exactly where they are.
>
> As matters stand, if *any* part of the savefile is corrupt on disk, 
> you lose the rest of the savefile - and those unique items if they 
> happen to be stored after it.  And let's face it, they usually are.

There are of course more robust binary formats than a simple memory
dump. One example that is as old as NetHack is the IFF format.

But what should NetHack do when it encounters a partly corrupt
savegame? Currently it just throws the whole savegame away.
 
> Secondly, the savefiles need a major redesign *anyway* for purposes 
> of both robustness and cross-platform compatibility. 
>
> There are a bunch of encoding tricks that could be used to make 
> savefiles many times more recoverable.  A file header section 
> giving byte offsets of anything that follows a variable-length 
> section would be a good start.  But going cross-platform with an
> ascii-encoded savefile with unique, unambiguous delimiters between 
> sections instead of writing binary data would be even better.

I would like to have automatic object serialization (so simple in modern
dynamic languages) but that is not easy in C.

> Of course it would be more robust.

Would it?

NetHacks save format is rather simple. Dump all the data in a simple
fashion. I don't think it is obvious that a ascii-encoded save format
would be more robust.

> But for another thing it would 
> allow people to continue games on machines other than the one on 
> which they started.  And for another, it would make the Hearse's 
> job ever so much easier.

A savegame format that works cross-platform or even only on different
machines must also be robust with NetHack programs compiled with
different compilation options.

